REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w06","title":"Softening, question tags, questions and embedded clauses","summary":"How to say things gently, so that they suit the listener and the context (softening); how English makes question tags and questions by moving the tense to the front; and how questions and that-clauses sit inside bigger sentences, where nothing moves.","n":6,"lectures":[{"code":"6.1","title":"Learning softening (knowing vs. using) | communicating with sensitivity & social awareness","video":"4cdnhtR06UI","seconds":2684},{"code":"6.2","title":"Question tags in english sentences | forming and using tag questions for natural conversation","video":"inEGIdgdOk4","seconds":1721},{"code":"6.3","title":"Questions in english (interrogative sentences)","video":"K4-RfkvOzXA","seconds":2088},{"code":"6.4","title":"Questions in english complex sentences (embedded sentences)","video":"qMwFLReD5ns","seconds":1851},{"code":"6.5","title":"Whether & if clauses in english sentences | usage, differences & examples","video":"U3fbo9xGlPQ","seconds":1166},{"code":"6.6","title":"That-clause in english sentences (complex/ embedded sentences) structure, function & examples","video":"81N4bFtxc7U","seconds":2740}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"softening-knowing-and-using","level":2,"title":"Softening: knowing and using"},{"id":"formal-and-informal-command-or-request","level":3,"title":"Formal and informal: command or request"},{"id":"softer-versions-of-a-request","level":3,"title":"Softer versions of a request"},{"id":"suggestions","level":3,"title":"Suggestions"},{"id":"discussing-an-issue","level":3,"title":"Discussing an issue"},{"id":"refusal-saying-no","level":3,"title":"Refusal: saying no"},{"id":"question-tags","level":2,"title":"Question tags"},{"id":"affirmative-and-negative-sentences","level":3,"title":"Affirmative and negative sentences"},{"id":"building-the-tag","level":3,"title":"Building the tag"},{"id":"why-we-use-tags","level":3,"title":"Why we use tags"},{"id":"the-influence-of-the-first-language","level":3,"title":"The influence of the first language"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-examples","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s examples"},{"id":"questions-tense-and-agreement-go-to-the-front","level":2,"title":"Questions: tense and agreement go to the front"},{"id":"making-a-question","level":3,"title":"Making a question"},{"id":"two-types-of-questions","level":3,"title":"Two types of questions"},{"id":"complex-sentences-and-embedded-questions","level":2,"title":"Complex sentences and embedded questions"},{"id":"that-the-complementizer","level":3,"title":"That: the complementizer"},{"id":"questions-inside-a-complex-sentence","level":3,"title":"Questions inside a complex sentence"},{"id":"embedded-questions-sound-polite","level":3,"title":"Embedded questions sound polite"},{"id":"whether-and-if-clauses","level":2,"title":"Whether and if clauses"},{"id":"where-only-whether-works","level":3,"title":"Where only whether works"},{"id":"which-verbs-take-these-clauses","level":3,"title":"Which verbs take these clauses"},{"id":"that-clauses-sentences-as-subjects-and-objects","level":2,"title":"That-clauses: sentences as subjects and objects"},{"id":"kinds-of-subjects","level":3,"title":"Kinds of subjects"},{"id":"kinds-of-objects","level":3,"title":"Kinds of objects"},{"id":"finite-and-non-finite-clauses","level":3,"title":"Finite and non-finite clauses"},{"id":"which-sentences-are-complex","level":3,"title":"Which sentences are complex?"},{"id":"that-clauses-as-subjects","level":3,"title":"That-clauses as subjects"},{"id":"verbs-with-two-objects","level":3,"title":"Verbs with two objects"},{"id":"where-the-two-objects-go","level":3,"title":"Where the two objects go"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Softening</strong> (6.1): a sentence must be <strong>accurate</strong> (grammatical) and <strong>appropriate</strong> (right for the listener and the context). Softer forms such as <em>Could we …?</em>, <em>I was wondering if …</em>, <em>If I were you, I would …</em>, <em>You seem to have …</em> and refusals without the word <em>no</em> keep the listener with you. There is no fixed rule: the context decides.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative sentences</strong> (6.2): the tense leaves the verb and comes before <em>not</em>: <em>I know</em> → <em>I don’t know</em>; <em>Raju confirmed</em> → <em>Raju did not confirm</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Question tags</strong> (6.2): <em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> The tag keeps the subject and the tense of the sentence and takes the opposite form: an affirmative sentence gets a negative tag, a negative sentence a positive tag. A negative tag must be contracted (<em>don’t</em>, <em>isn’t</em>, <em>didn’t</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Questions</strong> (6.3): English moves the <strong>tense and agreement</strong> to the front, with <em>do</em> to carry them when there is no other helper word: <em>Raju loves pizza</em> → <em>What does Raju love?</em> There are <strong>content</strong> (wh-) questions and <strong>yes-or-no</strong> questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Embedded questions</strong> (6.4): a <strong>complex sentence</strong> has a sentence as the object of its verb; two sentences joined by <em>and</em> do not make one. A question inside a complex sentence is no longer a question, so nothing moves: <em>I asked what you ate</em>, not ✗ <em>I asked what did you eat</em>. Such sentences also sound polite: <em>I wonder what her name is</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Whether and if</strong> (6.5) start indirect questions. Only <em>whether</em> is possible before a <em>to</em>-infinitive, after a preposition and right before <em>or not</em>. With <em>or</em> later in the sentence both work: <em>whether</em> is formal, <em>if</em> informal.</li>\n<li><strong>That-clauses</strong> (6.6): <em>that</em> can be dropped before a finite clause in the object position (never before a subject clause). An embedded clause is <strong>finite</strong> (it has tense) or <strong>non-finite</strong> (<em>to go</em>), and clauses can sit inside clauses (<strong>recursion</strong>). A <em>that</em>-clause can even be the subject, or the direct object of verbs like <em>tell</em> and <em>inform</em>. Heavy (sentence-long) objects go to the end.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"softening-knowing-and-using\">Softening: knowing and using</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Knowing a language and using it are two different things (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). <strong>Knowing</strong> is learning the structure: sounds, words, sentences and their details. That learning happens inside one person’s mind. <strong>Using</strong> always involves other people: one listener, a group or a large audience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Lecture 6.1 calls the skill of using language gently and with care for the listener <strong>softening</strong>. The lecturer warns that his examples are “tentative suggestions”: your own presence of mind and your feel for the society and the context will help you most (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>What “knowing” covers, from the slide “We know …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>What we know</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple sentence</td>\n<td>Sentence = Subject + Predicate; Predicate = Verb + Object(s) (adverbs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complex sentence</td>\n<td>Subject + Predicate [embedded sentence as an object]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreement</td>\n<td>person, number, gender</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tense</td>\n<td>present, past, future</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aspect</td>\n<td>habitual, continuous, perfective</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objects</strong> are required parts of the predicate; <strong>adverbs</strong> may not be required (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement</strong> is the matching of the verb with the subject’s number and person (gender matters little in English). Agreement, tense and aspect “work in a very intricate way” to join the subject and the predicate into a sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspect</strong> is how an action spreads over time: habitual (it happens regularly, <em>I read</em>), continuous (it is in progress, <em>I am reading</em>) or perfective (it is complete, <em>I have read</em>). The slide only names them; the examples are mine.</li>\n<li>A child who learns a language before about 15 years of age does all this automatically. After 15 we have to work at it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But the purpose of learning a language is to use it: to say what we want to say “properly, adequately and appropriately”. These three come from knowing the <strong>context</strong>, the situation in which we speak: who is listening, where, and why (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accuracy, appropriateness and impact (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy</strong> (also called <strong>grammaticality</strong>): the sentence follows the rules of grammar.</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriateness</strong>: the sentence is right for the context. The lecture calls appropriateness the key factor in the use of language.</li>\n<li>Accuracy and appropriateness together make us <strong>impactful</strong>. The slide “Being impactful …”: “We need to know how to say what we want to say. The way we use sentences makes all the difference.”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Grammar first, then softness</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Softening never replaces grammar. If we do not speak grammatically, “we leave a very strange impression” and we may lose the audience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). The lecturer’s rule: be “grammatically accurate plus communicatively appropriate”, and the second part “comes only after the first one” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which situations this is about</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture leaves aside listeners who are forced to listen, whatever the speaker says. It is about the times when you need the listener’s full attention and want your message received well (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). Knowing the context is “common sense in language”: children pick it up on their own, and adults learn it fast when they pay attention (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). Softening is also about how <strong>confident</strong> you sound, and confidence again needs both accuracy and appropriateness (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture takes four kinds of context. The question is always the same: how do we avoid sounding harsh to the listener? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>questions, suggestions and requests;</li>\n<li>opinions;</li>\n<li>issues (discussing a problem);</li>\n<li>refusal (saying no).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"formal-and-informal-command-or-request\">Formal and informal: command or request</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>There are two broad kinds of context: <strong>formal</strong> (for example, at work) and <strong>informal</strong> (for example, with friends). But “even in the most informal contexts, we still need to employ a bit of formality” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). We want to sound natural and effortless, “but we need to understand that it takes a lot of efforts to look effortless” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Imperative: command or request (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>imperative</strong> is a sentence that tells someone to do something. Its subject, <em>you</em>, is not said. An imperative can sound like a <strong>command</strong> (an order) or like a <strong>request</strong> (asking, with respect). The slide “Formal vs. Informal”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Give me a glass of water</em> is taken as a command, “not so respectful”.</li>\n<li><em>Please give me a glass of water</em> is taken as a request. <em>Please</em> is “a magical term in English”. It is fine in informal talk too, and “formally looking informal” makes you more impactful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n<li>With <em>Meet with me at 9 in the morning</em> and <em>Please meet with me at 9 in the morning</em>, which is which depends on the context.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Is <em>please</em> enough? (the lecture’s story at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The sentences: <em>Meet with me at 9 in the morning</em> and <em>Please meet with me at 9 in the morning</em>.</li>\n<li>A war room: the top army general ends a meeting with his lieutenants, officers who are junior to him. He says, <em>Please meet with me at 9 in the morning</em>. He could even say, <em>Is it possible for us to meet in the morning at nine?</em></li>\n<li>With army discipline, the lieutenants have no option to suggest another time. So every one of these sentences, even with <em>please</em>, remains a <strong>command</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A civil (non-military) office: your boss says either sentence. You still have to meet the boss, but you can now suggest an alternative: <em>Sir, is nine-thirty okay?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “Mere absence or presence of <em>please</em>” does not make a sentence a command or a request. “It is how we say and to whom we say in what context” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). These differences are “totally context-dependent”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"softer-versions-of-a-request\">Softer versions of a request</h3>\n<p>Instead of the imperative <em>Meet with me at 9 in the morning</em>, the slide “Softer version” offers three sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>How polite</th>\n<th>When the lecture uses it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Could we meet at 9 in the morning?</td>\n<td>polite</td>\n<td>in place of the imperative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Could we meet at 9 in the morning, please?</td>\n<td>more polite</td>\n<td>when you are not in a position to tell people to meet you; it makes the hearer think about your request “more sympathetically”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I was wondering if we could meet at 9 in the morning.</td>\n<td>excessively polite</td>\n<td>it gives the hearer an option</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>People who use the first sentence are not impolite. “We need to pick one out of the three, depending on the context” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The third one looks flexible, but the lecturer says it “buys you certainty”: the hearer is put in a position where saying no is hard (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In a situation without a strong <strong>hierarchy</strong> (an order of ranks, senior and junior), the higher degree of politeness “buys you more than you can actually get” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why these sound softer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Could we …?</em> turns an order into a question, using the polite modal <em>could</em> (<a href=\"#/english2/w05/can-and-could\">Week 5</a>). <em>I was wondering if …</em> goes one step further: the question is wrapped inside a statement, as an indirect question. Lectures 6.4 and 6.5 below show why such indirect questions “add mildness”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"suggestions\">Suggestions</h3>\n<p>A friend asks you, even in an informal situation, <em>Shall I invest in mutual funds?</em> (The lecturer stresses that this is about language, not about investing.) The slide “Suggestions …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No, you should not.</td>\n<td>fully grammatical, but “rather more abrupt than a suggestion”: too direct, and the listener may not be ready to take it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I think mutual funds are not doing good these days.</td>\n<td>no yes and no <em>no</em>; you give your opinion, which points towards no (spoken also as <em>I do not think mutual funds are doing good these days</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If I were you, I would not.</td>\n<td>more direct; “still sounds a little abrupt”, because it has a negative word in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If I were you, I would think several times about it.</td>\n<td>a “clear suggestion towards no”, but not direct; it allows the hearer options</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>Doing good</em> is the lecture’s wording; careful English says <em>doing well</em>.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Grammaticality is not enough (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A sentence is <strong>grammatical</strong> when it follows the rules of grammar.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>acceptable</strong> (appropriate) when it also fits the listener and the context.</li>\n<li>“Grammaticality is required, but it is not enough. Acceptability and appropriateness has to be topped up on grammaticality.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The softest answers do not impose: “You are not imposing your suggestion on your hearer. That is key in making ourselves softer” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"discussing-an-issue\">Discussing an issue</h3>\n<p>Someone has sent you a report, and when you meet, the person says (slide “Issues”, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Sir, I have submitted my report on accounts. Hope you liked it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecturer also says it as <em>Hope you had a chance to look at it</em>. The remark has two clear purposes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>to let you know (though you already know) that the job is done;</li>\n<li>to start a conversation when you run into each other: you say something the two of you share, rather than, say, a comment on the other person’s clothes. That itself is softening.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Hope you liked it</em> gives you an option; <em>Hope you had a chance to look at it</em> checks whether you have something to say (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>). Now compare the possible replies:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reply</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>You have made a mistake in calculations.</td>\n<td>grammatical, but “devastating in nature”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You have ruined it.</td>\n<td>grammatical, but devastating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You seem to have missed a few things. May I suggest a revisit?</td>\n<td>the same message, “loud and clear, but it is way softer”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is it possible for you to relook at this and send it to me again?</td>\n<td>(said aloud in the lecture) a polite question instead of a verdict</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It appears to me that the calculations do not add up. Could you please take a look at it again?</td>\n<td>a detailed comment on the report, still soft</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The harsh replies are not ungrammatical; they are wrong for the context, “because your purpose is not to devastate your listener” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>). The soft ones come from the slide “Softening …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Communicative competence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some people say that knowing the context is “extra-linguistic” (outside language). The lecturer disagrees. <strong>Communicative competence</strong>, knowing how to communicate, stay impactful and not lose people, must be one of our goals. The least we can aim for: at the end of a conversation, the hearer should not feel bad. “Even if it was not so pleasant, you can have a good conversation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"refusal-saying-no\">Refusal: saying no</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Saying no is “not difficult, but requires skill” (slide “Refusal … Saying no”). The skill is “to say no without having said no”, and it combines language skill with communicative confidence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>). The lecture’s two requests: <em>Can we go for a cup of tea?</em> and <em>Could you please bring me some storybooks on your way?</em> The second is “a very nice request”, but it asks for a favour (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Skilful refusal …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>). To <em>Can we go for a cup of tea?</em>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skilful refusal</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I have a lot of stuff pending to take care of.</td>\n<td>an affirmative sentence that “gives the sense of no”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I have a lot of stuff pending to take care of. Hope you won’t mind.</td>\n<td>mind what? The <em>no</em> that comes out of it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>To <em>Could you please bring me some storybooks on your way?</em>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skilful refusal</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I will try my best.</td>\n<td>short and grammatical, but “chances are you will not bring” them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The shop seems to have moved as I could not find it.</td>\n<td>it “says a lot”, but it is “more of no”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I am not sure if there is any bookstore on my way.</td>\n<td>“way more direct” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2430s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not lying: conveying the answer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>No, I cannot go for a cup of tea</em> and <em>No, I cannot bring them</em> are grammatical, but not appropriate, and “would not buy you much”. The lecturer adds that a refusal need not state the exact facts, yet he insists: “these are not the ways of lying, but these are ways of communication”, where the idea is to convey the sense of your answer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Things to notice …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>We need to be aware of the context.</li>\n<li>There is no specific rule for these things.</li>\n<li>Becoming softer in communication is a social skill.</li>\n<li>Combining the use of grammatical rules with social skills makes communication effective.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecturer’s exercise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You may already do much of this, and other people use such sentences with you. From now on, notice them. Make a list of situations, make a list of the possible answers in each, and see whether you can be more creative (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=2610s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 43:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Put the slide’s three softer versions in order, from polite to most polite: <em>I was wondering if we could meet at 9 in the morning.</em> / <em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning?</em> / <em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning, please?</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning?</em> is polite. A question replaces the order <em>Meet with me at 9</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning, please?</em> is more polite, because <em>please</em> is added.</li>\n<li><em>I was wondering if we could meet at 9 in the morning</em> is excessively polite. It is not even a direct question, and it gives the hearer an option.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>None of them is wrong: choose by context (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A friend asks <em>Shall I invest in mutual funds?</em> Why does the lecture prefer <em>If I were you, I would think several times about it</em> to <em>No, you should not</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>No, you should not</em> is grammatical but abrupt: it directs the friend, who may not be ready for it. <em>If I were you, I would not</em> is still a little abrupt, because of the negative word. <em>If I were you, I would think several times about it</em> points clearly towards no, but has no negative word and leaves the decision to the friend. It does not impose (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>Please meet with me at 9 in the morning</em> a request or a command?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>It depends on the context. From an army general to his lieutenants in a war room, it is a command: they cannot suggest another time. From a boss in a civil office, the meeting is still expected, but you can suggest an alternative: <em>Sir, is nine-thirty okay?</em> The word <em>please</em> alone does not decide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cdnhtR06UI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"question-tags\">Question tags</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> The short question at the end, <em>don’t you?</em>, is a <strong>question tag</strong> (also called a <strong>tag question</strong>). We use tags mostly in speech, sometimes in writing. Proper tags make speech “very impressive”. Without them a speaker is not only less grammatical but also less appropriate: people still get the message, but the speech loses something (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). To build tags you need two things you already know: <strong>agreement</strong> and <strong>tense</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Declarative Sentence” recalls the parts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>declarative</strong> sentence makes a statement; it is not a question or an order. Tags are added to declarative sentences.</li>\n<li>Sentence = [Subject + Predicate]. The two parts are joined by <strong>agreement</strong> (the verb matches the subject’s person and number: <em>Raju beats</em>, but <em>they beat</em>) and by <strong>tense</strong> (the time form of the verb: present, past, future).</li>\n<li>Predicate = [Verb + Object(s) [adjuncts/adverbs]].</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"affirmative-and-negative-sentences\">Affirmative and negative sentences</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Affirmative and negative (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>affirmative</strong> (positive) sentence says that something is so: <em>I know what I am doing.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>negative</strong> sentence says that it is not so, with <em>not</em> (or <em>never</em>): <em>I don’t know what I am doing.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Two types” puts each affirmative sentence next to its negative:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Affirmative (tense)</th>\n<th>Negative</th>\n<th>Where the tense is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I know what I am doing. (present)</td>\n<td>I don’t know what I am doing.</td>\n<td>it left <em>know</em>; <em>do</em> carries it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They have had food. (present)</td>\n<td>They have not had food.</td>\n<td>already outside the verb, on <em>have</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju confirmed that he will be joining the discussion. (past)</td>\n<td>Raju did not confirm that he will be joining the discussion.</td>\n<td>it left <em>confirmed</em>; <em>did</em> carries it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju was going to speak with his friends. (past)</td>\n<td>Raju was not going to speak with his friends.</td>\n<td>already outside the verb, on <em>was</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tense comes out of the verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>In an affirmative sentence the tense sits on the verb. In the present we often cannot see it (<em>know</em>); in the past we can (<em>confirm<strong>ed</strong></em>).</li>\n<li>When the sentence becomes negative, the tense “gets out of the verb”. The order becomes <strong>tense + negation + verb</strong>, and the verb is left without tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li>When the tense is already outside the verb (<em>have</em>, <em>was</em>), <em>not</em> simply comes after it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In short: in a negative sentence, “tense is out of the verb, and tense precedes negation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Raju confirmed …</em> made negative (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The verb <em>confirmed</em> carries the past tense (<em>-ed</em>).</li>\n<li>Negation pulls the past out of the verb, which becomes plain <em>confirm</em>.</li>\n<li>The past tense cannot stand alone, so the helper word <em>do</em> carries it: <em>do</em> + past = <em>did</em>.</li>\n<li>The order is tense + <em>not</em> + verb: <em>did not confirm</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Raju did not confirm that he will be joining the discussion.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why look so closely? This is how the mind computes a sentence. Paying attention to it makes our speech accurate, and “when this accuracy combines with confidence, we get fluency” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"building-the-tag\">Building the tag</h3>\n<p>The slide “Tag Questions”: a tag “appears at the end of the sentence” and “appears in the form of a question” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). The sentence ends with a comma instead of a full stop, and the tag ends with a question mark.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The structure of a tag (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A tag is built from its own sentence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Same subject</strong>, as a pronoun (<em>Raju</em> → <em>he</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Same tense</strong>, with agreement. As in every question, the tense word comes first: <em>do/does/did</em>, or the sentence’s own <em>is</em>, <em>was</em>, <em>will</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Opposite form</strong>: an affirmative sentence takes a <strong>negative</strong> tag; a negative sentence takes an <strong>affirmative</strong> (positive) tag.</li>\n<li><strong>Contraction</strong>: in a negative tag, the tense and <em>not</em> must be joined into one word: <em>don’t</em>, <em>isn’t</em>, <em>didn’t</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The sentence <em>You speak English</em> is affirmative, so the tag is negative.</li>\n<li>Subject: <em>you</em>. The tag keeps <em>you</em>.</li>\n<li>Tense: present. A question fronts the tense; <em>speak</em> has no helper word, so <em>do</em> carries it.</li>\n<li>Make it negative: <em>do</em> + <em>not</em>.</li>\n<li>Contract, as the tag requires: <em>do not</em> → <em>don’t</em>.</li>\n<li>Tense first, then subject: <em>don’t you?</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> For the negative sentence the tag is positive: <em>You do not speak English, do you?</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Contraction is optional in the sentence, compulsory in the tag</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the sentence itself both forms are fine: <em>You do not speak English</em> or <em>You don’t speak English</em>. <strong>Contracted</strong> means that two elements, here the tense (<em>do</em>) and the negation (<em>not</em>), are joined into one word. In the tag, contraction is <strong>mandatory</strong>: ✗ <em>You speak English, do not you?</em> ✓ <em>don’t you?</em>; ✗ <em>He is devastated with the outcome, is not he?</em> ✓ <em>isn’t he?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>) (Very formal English can leave <em>not</em> whole, but then it comes after the subject: <em>do you not?</em>, <em>is he not?</em> <em>Do not you?</em> and <em>is not he?</em> are always wrong.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Structure of Tag Questions in English” adds the past (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>You liked traveling, didn’t you?</em> The past comes out as <em>did</em>, and <em>did</em> + <em>not</em> = <em>didn’t</em>.</li>\n<li><em>You did not like traveling, did you?</em> Here the tense is already out of the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you say <em>You liked traveling</em>, you ask the listener to confirm it with a negative tag, <em>didn’t you?</em> The listener confirms it (<em>Yes, I did</em>) or corrects you (<em>No, I didn’t</em>). (The lecturer’s sample reply, “no, no, no, I liked travelling”, would confuse a listener: in English, <em>yes</em> goes with <em>I did</em> and <em>no</em> with <em>I didn’t</em>.) When you say <em>You did not like traveling</em>, the tag is <em>did you?</em>, and the listener answers yes or no accordingly (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-use-tags\">Why we use tags</h3>\n<p>The slide “Functions of Tag Questions” lists three basic functions; “there could be more” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engage</strong>: draw the listener into the conversation.</li>\n<li><strong>Verify</strong>: check that something is true.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirm</strong>: get the listener to agree with what you said.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-influence-of-the-first-language\">The influence of the first language</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">First language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Your <strong>first language</strong> is the language you acquired effortlessly as a child, without much instruction, from the people around you: parents, family, friends, school and neighbours.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>All languages have tags, so check how yours makes them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). We often carry our first language’s habit into English. The slide “Influence of our first language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>You speak English, right?</em></li>\n<li><em>You speak English, isn’t it?</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These meet the same function (to verify and confirm), and people understand them: “you get the sense of what people want to say”. But the appropriate tag is <em>You speak English, don’t you?</em> The influence of the first language is natural, with “nothing wrong about it”; you reduce it by paying attention (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-examples\">The lecture’s examples</h3>\n<p>From the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence and tag</th>\n<th>Tense word in the tag</th>\n<th>Sentence → tag</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>You don’t know the consequence, do you?</td>\n<td><em>do</em> (present)</td>\n<td>negative → positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He is devastated with the outcome, isn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>is</em> (present)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He rushed as fast as he could, didn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>did</em> (past)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whenever we play chess, Raju beats me, doesn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>does</em> (present; <em>Raju</em> is third person singular)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He spoke with you, didn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>did</em> (past)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She wants me to come to Chennai, doesn’t she?</td>\n<td><em>does</em> (present, third person singular)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He was dead by the time they found him, wasn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>was</em> (past)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He called me names, didn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>did</em> (past)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He broke his leg while playing football, didn’t he?</td>\n<td><em>did</em> (past)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She never misses an opportunity to make me feel bad, does she?</td>\n<td><em>does</em> (present, third person singular)</td>\n<td>negative (<em>never</em>) → positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They asked him, didn’t they?</td>\n<td><em>did</em> (past)</td>\n<td>affirmative → negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He will not allow this, will he?</td>\n<td><em>will</em> (future)</td>\n<td>negative → positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The slide’s misprint: <em>Raju beats me, doesn’t she?</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide prints <em>Whenever we play chess, Raju beats me, doesn’t she?</em> The lecturer says it correctly: <em>Raju always beats me, doesn’t he?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>). Raju is a man, so the pronoun is <em>he</em>. The tag follows the main clause, <em>Raju beats me</em>. Because <em>Raju</em> is third person singular and the tense is present, <em>do</em> becomes <em>does</em>: “along with tense we need to pay attention to agreement”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Never is negative</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Never</em> makes a sentence negative even without <em>not</em>, so the tag is positive: <em>She never misses an opportunity …, does she?</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Things to notice …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=inEGIdgdOk4&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is important to work on tag questions to sound impressive.</li>\n<li>Negation and tense (the lecturer adds in ink: + agreement).</li>\n<li>Contraction in tag questions is a requirement.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Add the tag: <em>They have had food, ___?</em> (built from the lecture’s sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <em>they</em>.</li>\n<li>Tense: present, carried by <em>have</em> (the tense is already outside the main verb <em>had</em>).</li>\n<li>The sentence is affirmative, so the tag is negative: <em>have</em> + <em>not</em>.</li>\n<li>Contract: <em>haven’t</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>They have had food, haven’t they?</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is wrong with <em>You speak English, isn’t it?</em> and <em>He is devastated with the outcome, is not he?</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>isn’t it?</em> does not repeat the sentence’s subject (<em>you</em>) or its tense word (present, so <em>do</em>). It is a habit carried over from the first language: people understand it, but the appropriate tag is <em>don’t you?</em></li>\n<li><em>is not he?</em> is not contracted. In a tag, tense and <em>not</em> must be joined: <em>isn’t he?</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make <em>Raju was going to speak with his friends</em> negative, then add the tag.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>The tense is already outside the main verb, on <em>was</em>, so <em>not</em> comes right after it: <em>Raju was not going to speak with his friends.</em></li>\n<li>Tag: subject <em>Raju</em> → <em>he</em>; tense past, on <em>was</em>; the sentence is negative, so the tag is positive: <em>was he?</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Raju was not going to speak with his friends, was he?</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"questions-tense-and-agreement-go-to-the-front\">Questions: tense and agreement go to the front</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.3 explains how English makes questions. Two “invisible” parts of every sentence do the work: <strong>agreement</strong> and <strong>tense</strong>. Once you see how they move, you see the system behind sentences, and why the mind “can produce unlimited number of sentences once it has got its machinery fixed” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence = [Subject + Predicate]. A sentence is not possible without either of them, and <strong>agreement</strong> connects them.</li>\n<li>Predicate = [Verb + Object(s) [adjuncts/adverbs]]. Objects, the complements of the verb, appear or not depending on the verb; adjuncts are not essential.</li>\n<li>Order of words: <strong>subject, verb, object</strong>. In English, “objects follow the verbs” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Verbs host agreement marking as well as tense. Agreement and tense are <strong>functional elements</strong>: words or endings that do a grammatical job. They are “invisible”: sometimes they appear in a sentence, sometimes not (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Agreement (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Three features can take part in agreement: <strong>person</strong>, <strong>number</strong> and <strong>gender</strong>. In English only person and number matter, so the slide sets gender aside.</li>\n<li><strong>Person</strong>: first (<em>I, we</em>), second (<em>you</em>), third (<em>he, she, it, they</em>, and any noun such as <em>Ramu</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Number</strong>: singular (one) or plural (more than one). “Every noun must be either singular or plural in English.”</li>\n<li>These features belong to the subject; the matching values appear on the verb. That matching is <strong>agreement</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Ramu loves cricket</em> (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The subject <em>Ramu</em> carries no visible mark. We infer that he is <strong>third person, singular</strong>.</li>\n<li>The verb <em>love</em> “hosts matching information”: it takes the ending <em>-s</em>.</li>\n<li>That <em>-s</em> marks third person singular, and also the present tense.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Ramu = III person/singular</em>; <em>loves</em> shows the agreement.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Tense” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tense</th>\n<th>Singular</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n<th>Only with <em>I</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present</td>\n<td>is</td>\n<td>are</td>\n<td>am (not even with <em>we</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past</td>\n<td>was</td>\n<td>were</td>\n<td>no special form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future</td>\n<td>shall / will</td>\n<td>shall / will</td>\n<td>no special form</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>These words are taught as <strong>auxiliaries</strong> (helping verbs), forms of the verb <em>be</em>. The lecturer prefers to call them <strong>tense markers</strong>, which also carry agreement, and says: “they are not verbs” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Am</em> is special: it goes only with the first person singular, <em>I</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). (<em>Are</em> also goes with <em>you</em>, even for one person.)</li>\n<li><em>Shall</em> and <em>will</em> can be used with all three persons, singular and plural. The lecturer says modern English makes no distinction between them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li>These words carry the tense only when they are in the sentence. Without them, the tense sits on the verb: <em>Ramu loves cricket</em> (present; the <em>-s</em> also marks singular), <em>Raju loved cricket</em> (past, <em>-ed</em>). In the plural present there is no mark at all: tense is “just zero” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"making-a-question\">Making a question</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interrogative sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A question is also called an <strong>interrogative sentence</strong>. The lecturer insists that names are not important: what matters is how questions work.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Raju loves pizza</em> → <em>What does Raju love?</em> (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The statement: <em>Raju loves pizza</em>. The <em>-s</em> on <em>loves</em> carries tense (present) and agreement (third person singular).</li>\n<li>We ask about the object, <em>pizza</em>. Its place becomes empty: <em>Raju love ____</em>. <em>Love</em> still needs an object; the empty place is what we ask about.</li>\n<li>Tense and agreement “get separated from their host verb” and move out, to the front (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). The verb is left in its <strong>bare form</strong> (no ending): <em>love</em>.</li>\n<li>The <em>-s</em> is only a <strong>morpheme</strong> (the smallest meaningful part of a word). It “cannot stand on its own”, so English uses <em>do</em> to carry it: <em>do</em> + present + third person singular = <em>does</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The question word comes first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>What does Raju love?</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s present-tense and past-tense slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statement</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What went to the front</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju loves pizza.</td>\n<td>What does Raju love?</td>\n<td>present + agreement, carried by <em>do</em>: <em>does</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju eats thin pizza in the evening.</td>\n<td>What does Raju eat in the evening?</td>\n<td>the same; the adjunct <em>in the evening</em> stays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju is coming to Chennai tomorrow.</td>\n<td>Is Raju coming to Chennai tomorrow?</td>\n<td><em>is</em> itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju loved pizza.</td>\n<td>What did Raju love?</td>\n<td>past, carried by <em>do</em>: <em>did</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju ate thin pizza in the evening.</td>\n<td>What did Raju eat in the evening?</td>\n<td>the past inside <em>ate</em>, carried by <em>did</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju was coming to Chennai.</td>\n<td>Was Raju coming to Chennai?</td>\n<td><em>was</em> itself</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The rule of questions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Tense plus agreement gets fronted to form the question.” When the sentence has its own tense word (<em>is</em>, <em>was</em>), that word moves. When the tense is on the verb, <em>do</em> carries it: <strong>does = do + present</strong>, <strong>did = do + past</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Verbs with their own past form</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Love</em> makes its past with <em>-ed</em> (<em>loved</em>). <em>Eat</em> has its own past form, <em>ate</em>, and <em>read</em> looks the same in the past. They show no past marker, but a question proves the past is inside: <em>Raju ate …</em> → <em>What did Raju eat?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-types-of-questions\">Two types of questions</h3>\n<p>The slide “Things to notice …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>): question formation “attracts attention in the structure of a sentence”; questions are fronted in English (in ink: tense + agreement); there are mainly two types.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Content type and yes-or-no type (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>content-type</strong> question uses a question word: <em>who, what, when, where, how, why</em>. To answer it you must give content (information): <em>What does Raju love?</em> → <em>Pizza.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>yes-or-no</strong> question can be answered with yes or no. It begins with the tense marker: <em>Was Raju coming to Chennai?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>All English question words begin with <em>wh</em>, so they are called <strong>wh-words</strong>; <em>how</em> has the <em>w</em> and <em>h</em> too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Questions” asks you to check the question words of your own language, and their position (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>). Take Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada, Hindi, Punjabi, Bangla, Assamese, Odia or Marathi: you will see that in English the question word comes at the start and the tense is fronted, “unlike our languages” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>). For example (mine), Hindi says <em>Raju kya khata hai?</em>, word for word “Raju what eats?”: the question word stays where the object stands, and nothing moves to the front.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why compare languages?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Our main language strongly affects a language we learn after about 15 years of age. Noticing such differences sets “certain parameters” in the mind: in English, the tense must come first. There are no separate compartments in the mind for each language; “learning of new language always works on exploiting existing structure” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask, do not interrogate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4-RfkvOzXA&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Questions are called interrogative sentences, but in speech a question must not sound like you are <strong>interrogating</strong> the person (questioning them hard, like an investigator). Ask to get information, and find ways that do not “directly hit the person”. This is <strong>pragmatics</strong>, the knowledge of how language is used in context, and we must train ourselves in it. The next two lectures give such ways: <em>I wonder what her name is</em>, <em>Could you tell me if the doctor is available?</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make a question about the object: <em>Raju eats thin pizza in the evening.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>The object <em>thin pizza</em> is asked about, so the question word is <em>what</em>.</li>\n<li>Tense and agreement (present, third person singular) leave <em>eats</em>, so the verb becomes <em>eat</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Do</em> carries them: <em>does</em>. It comes right after the question word.</li>\n<li>The adjunct <em>in the evening</em> stays where it was.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>What does Raju eat in the evening?</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Content type or yes-or-no type? <em>Was Raju coming to Chennai?</em> and <em>What did Raju love?</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Was Raju coming to Chennai?</em> is a yes-or-no question. It can be answered with yes or no, and it begins with the tense marker <em>was</em>.</li>\n<li><em>What did Raju love?</em> is a content question. The answer must give content (<em>pizza</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why <em>did</em> in <em>What did Raju eat?</em>, when <em>Raju ate thin pizza</em> has no <em>-ed</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Ate</em> is the past form of <em>eat</em>, with the past hidden inside it. In the question the past leaves the verb, so the verb becomes <em>eat</em>, and the past needs a carrier: <em>do</em> + past = <em>did</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"complex-sentences-and-embedded-questions\">Complex sentences and embedded questions</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.3 fronted the tense to make a question. Lecture 6.4 asks what happens when a question sits <strong>inside</strong> a bigger sentence. First it pins down what makes a sentence complex. The lecture’s promise: once you see how the mind builds a sentence inside a sentence, you speak with more confidence and more accuracy (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Sentence and Complex Sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence = [Subject + Predicate], joined by agreement: the subject’s number and person match the features on the verb.</li>\n<li>Predicate = [Verb + Object(s) [adjuncts/adverbs]].</li>\n<li>Complex sentence: Subject + Predicate [embedded sentence as object].</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whether a sentence has objects is “totally contingent upon the nature of the verb” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Verb type</th>\n<th>Objects</th>\n<th>Lecture example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>intransitive</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>I sleep early.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>transitive</td>\n<td>one</td>\n<td>I like pizza. (<em>pizza</em> is the object, or complement, of <em>like</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ditransitive</td>\n<td>two</td>\n<td>I teach English to the students at IIT Madras.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In the ditransitive example, <em>English</em> is the <strong>direct object</strong> (teach what?), <em>to the students</em> is the <strong>indirect object</strong> (teach to whom? the person who receives) and <em>at IIT Madras</em> is an adjunct (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complex sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A sentence is <strong>complex</strong> when its predicate contains another sentence as the <strong>object of the verb</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The inner sentence is called an <strong>embedded sentence</strong> or <strong>subordinate clause</strong>. “A subordinate clause is a sentence”, embedded in a particular way: as the object of a verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The outer sentence is the <strong>matrix</strong> sentence (“matrix means main one”). So: main clause = matrix clause; subordinate clause = embedded clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Some verbs can take a whole sentence as their object: <em>know, wish, wonder</em> and many more; “there is no specific list” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>I wonder what her name is</em> (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <em>I</em>.</li>\n<li>Verb: <em>wonder</em>, a verb that needs a whole sentence as its object.</li>\n<li>Object: <em>what her name is</em>. The slide writes it as Sub – I, V – wonder [S], where S stands for sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a complex sentence. <em>I wonder what her name is</em> is the matrix; <em>what her name is</em> is the subordinate clause, embedded as the object of <em>wonder</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">A complex sentence is a simple sentence in disguise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A complex sentence is also essentially a simple sentence because it does not violate the requirements of a sentence.” It still has a subject, a verb and an object; only the object is a sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). Understand this and you understand “a big chunk” of how the mind computes sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two sentences joined by <em>and</em> are not complex</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Raju likes pizza and Ramu likes sandwich</em> is two sentences <strong>coordinated</strong> (joined) by the conjunction <em>and</em>. Neither is the object of a verb in the other, so it is not a complex sentence. When two people like two different things, the second <em>likes</em> can even be dropped: <em>Raju likes pizza and Ramu ___ sandwich</em> (the slide marks the gap with a red <em>xxx</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). (Standard English would say <em>sandwiches</em> or <em>a sandwich</em>; the point about <em>and</em> is the same.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"that-the-complementizer\">That: the complementizer</h3>\n<p>The slide “Complex Sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>I wish (that) I had a lot of money. / I wish I had a lot of money.</li>\n<li>John knows (that) Peter likes Mary. / John knows Peter likes Mary.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Test whether the clause is the object by asking “what?”: I wish <strong>what</strong>? We can wish for a noun (<em>I wish money. I wish happiness.</em>) or for a whole sentence, <em>that I had a lot of money</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). (The lecturer’s noun examples are not standard English: say <em>I wish for money</em>, or use two objects, <em>I wish you happiness</em>. The test still works: <em>wish</em> takes an object.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complementizer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A whole sentence that is the object of a verb is also a <strong>complement</strong> of that verb. The word that introduces such a bigger complement is a <strong>complementizer</strong>: here <em>that</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It “does not have any literal meaning”; it is just a functional element.</li>\n<li>Sometimes it is optional: <em>I wish I had a lot of money</em>, <em>John knows Peter likes Mary</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li>When it can be dropped and when it must stay follows “straightforward rules”, which the lecture leaves for later (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"questions-inside-a-complex-sentence\">Questions inside a complex sentence</h3>\n<p>First a recap from the slide “Questions in a simple sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>): <em>What does Raju love?</em> (present) and <em>What did Raju love?</em> (past) are content questions; <em>Is Raju coming to Chennai tomorrow?</em> is a yes-or-no question. In each, a question word may come first, and tense and agreement are extracted from the verb and fronted.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">No fronting inside a complex sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“When question sentences are embedded within a larger sentence, then there is no fronting of tense and agreement.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Questions in a Complex sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). The slide marks wrong sentences with a star (*); this page uses ✗.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Direct question</th>\n<th>Inside a bigger sentence</th>\n<th>Wrong</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What did you eat in the evening?</td>\n<td>I asked what you ate in the evening.</td>\n<td>✗ I asked what did you eat in the evening.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What did you want?</td>\n<td>I know what you wanted.</td>\n<td>✗ I know what did you want.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>I asked what you ate in the evening</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Direct question: <em>What did you eat in the evening?</em> The past is fronted as <em>did</em>, and the verb is bare: <em>eat</em>.</li>\n<li>Make it the object of <em>asked</em>: <em>I asked [ … ]</em>.</li>\n<li>Inside the brackets it is no longer a question. The whole sentence is a statement about what I asked: “It is a simple declarative sentence, hence no extraction” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li>So the past stays on the verb (<em>ate</em>), and the order is subject + verb: <em>you ate</em>.</li>\n<li><em>What</em> stays at the start of the embedded clause.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>I asked what you ate in the evening.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A common mistake in Indian English</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Many times, when we speak English in India, we make this mistake”: ✗ <em>I asked what did you eat in the evening</em>. Fixing it is “one such big thing that we need to fix to make our English more impressive and effective” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). The lecturer asks you to understand the rule rather than memorize it: extraction of tense and agreement happens <strong>only in a question sentence</strong>, and an embedded question is not a question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"embedded-questions-sound-polite\">Embedded questions sound polite</h3>\n<p>The embedded form is also a softening tool (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Direct</th>\n<th>Less direct (embedded)</th>\n<th>What changes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What is her name?</td>\n<td>I wonder what her name is.</td>\n<td>still shows your curiosity, but it is no longer a direct question; <em>is</em> is not fronted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the doctor available?</td>\n<td>Could you tell me if the doctor is available?</td>\n<td>the main clause is a question (<em>Could</em> is fronted), but the embedded clause keeps the order <em>the doctor is</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What did you want?</td>\n<td>I know what you wanted.</td>\n<td>a statement instead of a question</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>What is her name?</em> is grammatical, but even to a friend it is “too direct a question” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>). <em>Is the doctor available?</em> is a perfectly good yes-or-no question; the embedded form simply makes it less direct (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>). Functionally, complex sentences are “used as a marker of politeness”; structurally, “a complex sentence is also essentially a simple sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Things to notice …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A complex sentence has an embedded sentence as an object of the verb in the matrix predicate.</li>\n<li>A coordinated sentence is not a complex sentence.</li>\n<li>A question sentence that is embedded does not have extraction of tense out of the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct the sentence: <em>I know what did you want.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The question <em>What did you want?</em> is now the object of <em>know</em>, so it is not a question any more. No tense moves to the front: the past stays on the verb, and the order is subject + verb.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>I know what you wanted.</em> (The slide marks the wrong form with a star at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qMwFLReD5ns&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>I wish I had a lot of money</em> a complex sentence, and where is the complementizer?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Yes. Subject <em>I</em>, verb <em>wish</em>, and the object is the whole sentence <em>I had a lot of money</em> (I wish what?). The complementizer <em>that</em> has been dropped, which is allowed here: <em>I wish (that) I had a lot of money</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make <em>Is the doctor available?</em> less direct, as the lecture does.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Could you tell me if the doctor is available?</em> The direct question becomes an embedded clause after <em>tell me</em>, introduced by <em>if</em>. Inside it there is no fronting: <em>the doctor is</em>, not <em>is the doctor</em>. Only the main clause, <em>Could you tell me …?</em>, is a question.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"whether-and-if-clauses\">Whether and if clauses</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.5 looks at two more words that start embedded clauses: <em>whether</em> and <em>if</em>. They let a yes-or-no question sit inside a bigger sentence. Compare the direct question <em>Will she come?</em> (my example) with the lecture’s <em>I don’t know whether she will come</em>. Knowing their small differences helps you use “multiple types of sentences” for impressive speaking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Sentence” recalls the basics (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sentence = [Subject + Predicate], joined by agreement (person, number, gender) and tense, “invisible functional elements”.</li>\n<li><em>Ramu likes Tamil movies.</em> <em>Like</em> is transitive; <em>Tamil movies</em> is its object.</li>\n<li><em>Raju sleeps on the floor.</em> <em>Sleep</em> is intransitive; <em>on the floor</em> is just an adverb, and there is no object (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complement clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In a complex sentence the verb’s object (its complement) is a sentence instead of a noun phrase like <em>Tamil movies</em>. That sentence is a <strong>complement clause</strong>, embedded in the predicate.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I know [what I am doing].</em></li>\n<li><em>Raju confirmed [that he will be joining the discussion].</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Verbs like <em>know, confirm, ask, understand</em> can take either a noun phrase or a sentence as their complement; the lecturer writes them as know [NP/S], confirm [NP/S]. “Not all verbs can take a sentence as a complement” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Whether and if clauses” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Both function as <strong>complementizers</strong>: “elements in English which can introduce a complement”.</li>\n<li>As a clause, they appear at the <strong>end</strong> of the sentence. Sentential complements are <strong>heavy complements</strong>, and heavy complements come at the end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li>They appear in the form of an <strong>indirect question</strong>, a question wrapped inside a statement, so they “add mildness” and help us soften our language. They are handy when you do not want to ask someone a very direct question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>They express doubts, or choices between alternatives (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide “Whether and if” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>What the clause does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I don’t know whether she will come.</td>\n<td>know</td>\n<td>expresses uncertainty, doubt: I am not sure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She asked me if I was coming.</td>\n<td>ask</td>\n<td>checks, makes sure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I cannot decide whether to accept the new job offer.</td>\n<td>decide</td>\n<td>an infinitival clause (<em>to accept</em>): only <em>whether</em>, ✗ <em>if</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A <strong>to-infinitive</strong> is <em>to</em> + the bare verb: <em>to accept</em>, <em>to get</em>. A clause built on it, with no tense, is an <strong>infinitival</strong> (non-finite) clause.</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-only-whether-works\">Where only whether works</h3>\n<p>The slides “Differences between whether and if” and the one after it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Which word</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>before a <em>to</em>-infinitive</td>\n<td>only <em>whether</em></td>\n<td>I’m not sure whether to get a new laptop.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>after a preposition</td>\n<td>only <em>whether</em></td>\n<td>There are doubts about whether the decision was fair.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>right before <em>or not</em></td>\n<td>only <em>whether</em></td>\n<td>The question is whether or not we have the right to interfere.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>or</em> later in the sentence</td>\n<td>both: <em>whether</em> (formal, preferable) or <em>if</em> (very informal)</td>\n<td>It is not clear whether / if the source of information is reliable or not.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In <em>There are doubts about whether the decision was fair</em>, the clause is the complement of the noun <em>doubts</em> (after the preposition <em>about</em>), not of a verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). So these clauses need not always be complements of the predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>It is not clear whether the source of information is reliable</em> is fully grammatical without <em>or not</em>; adding <em>or not</em> makes the sentence formal and complete. “The use of <em>if</em> makes the context informal and the use of <em>whether</em> keeps it formal” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Choosing between whether and if</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Whether</em> works in every example on these slides and keeps the sentence formal. <em>If</em> works only when none of the three limits applies (no <em>to</em>-infinitive after it, no preposition before it, no <em>or not</em> right after it), and it sounds informal.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"which-verbs-take-these-clauses\">Which verbs take these clauses</h3>\n<p>The slide “Nature of predicate” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>) shows predicates that take such clauses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>She <strong>asked</strong> if I liked dosa.</li>\n<li>I <strong>can’t tell</strong> whether she likes me or not.</li>\n<li>I <strong>was unsure</strong> whether my husband would actually come with me.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Not every verb that takes a complement clause can take whether or if type of complement clause” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). For example (mine): <em>I believe that he is innocent</em> is fine, but ✗ <em>I believe whether he is innocent</em> is not.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Patterns, not lists</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Speakers who acquired English as their first language know these limits by intuition. Learners after 15 years of age need practice. If we look for the patterns the way children do, which structures are possible and which are not, “we get very close to the native intuition” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Things to notice …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Whether</em> and <em>if</em> can be complementizers.</li>\n<li>They introduce a clause.</li>\n<li>They start embedded clauses.</li>\n<li>There are more functions of these two words in English.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>Whether</em> or <em>if</em>? (a) <em>I cannot decide ___ to accept the new job offer.</em> (b) <em>There are doubts about ___ the decision was fair.</em> (c) <em>The question is ___ or not we have the right to interfere.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>All three take <strong>whether</strong> only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) a <em>to</em>-infinitive follows;</li>\n<li>(b) it comes after the preposition <em>about</em>;</li>\n<li>(c) <em>or not</em> comes right after it.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Can <em>if</em> replace <em>whether</em> in <em>It is not clear whether the source of information is reliable or not</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Yes, because <em>or</em> comes later in the sentence. Both are grammatical, but <em>whether</em> is formal and preferable, while <em>if</em> makes the sentence very informal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U3fbo9xGlPQ&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"that-clauses-sentences-as-subjects-and-objects\">That-clauses: sentences as subjects and objects</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lectures 6.4 and 6.5 put a sentence in the object slot with <em>what</em>, <em>if</em> and <em>whether</em>. Lecture 6.6 turns to the clause that begins with <em>that</em>, the <strong>that-clause</strong>. Where can it stand? As the object of a verb (the usual place), as the second object of a verb with two objects, and even as the subject. So the lecture first lists the kinds of subjects and objects a sentence can have (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Sentence and Complex Sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One sentence has one subject and one predicate, joined by agreement.</li>\n<li>The verb is the most crucial part of the predicate, because the nature of the verb decides how many objects there are: from 0 to 2 (written in ink on the slide). Adjuncts give extra information about the verb.</li>\n<li>Complex sentence: Subject + Predicate [embedded sentence as an object]. The lecturer writes “that-clause” under it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The subject is an independent part of the sentence; objects depend on the verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"kinds-of-subjects\">Kinds of subjects</h3>\n<p>The lecturer writes these on a blank slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind of subject</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>What to notice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>hidden <em>you</em></td>\n<td>(You) sit down, please.</td>\n<td>an imperative: the subject is <em>you</em> (second person), but it is not said</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>expletive <em>it</em></td>\n<td>It is raining. It is warm. It is cold.</td>\n<td><em>it</em> means nothing; it only fills the subject place</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>expletive <em>there</em></td>\n<td>There are many students in the class.</td>\n<td>a long sentence, but its subject “has zero meaning”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a noun (phrase)</td>\n<td>Raju likes pizza.</td>\n<td>the ordinary subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a whole sentence</td>\n<td>That Raju delivered a good lecture pleased Ramu.</td>\n<td>new in this lecture (below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Expletive (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>expletive</strong> is a word that is “semantically vacuous”: it has no meaning in the sentence and only fills the subject position. English uses <em>it</em> and <em>there</em> this way. English needs them because “in English, subject must be overtly present” (actually said or written).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two rules about subjects</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Imperative sentences in all languages leave the subject out, because it can only be <em>you</em>. “When it is so obvious, we do not need to articulate it”: the <strong>principle of economy</strong> (<a href=\"#/english2/w03/imperatives-have-a-hidden-subject\">Week 3</a>). The subject is still there; it is just not overt.</li>\n<li>English also requires every sentence to have a subject. When a sentence has no natural subject, as with the weather, an expletive fills the place (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"kinds-of-objects\">Kinds of objects</h3>\n<p>On the blank slide and the slide “Simple vs. Complex Sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind of object</th>\n<th>Lecture examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a noun phrase</td>\n<td>Raju likes <strong>pizza</strong>. Raju reads <strong>a story</strong> before bedtime. I want <strong>money</strong>. I want <strong>peace</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an infinitive (non-finite) clause</td>\n<td>I want <strong>to go</strong>. I want <strong>him to go</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <em>that</em>-clause (a full sentence)</td>\n<td>I wish <strong>that I had a lot of money</strong>. John knows <strong>that Peter likes Mary</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Pizza</em> and <em>a story</em> are not just nouns but <strong>noun phrases</strong>: a noun with its small words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). <em>Before bedtime</em> is an adjunct.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>I wish (that) I had a lot of money</em> (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Subject: <em>I</em>. Verb: <em>wish</em>.</li>\n<li>Wish what? <em>That I had a lot of money</em>: this is the object. The slide’s pattern: S V O, with the O itself a sentence (a that-clause).</li>\n<li>The object is also a complement of the verb, and <em>that</em> introduces the whole complement. So <em>that</em> is the complementizer, “comp” for short (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Drop <em>that</em>: <em>I wish I had a lot of money</em>. This is allowed because the clause is a full <strong>finite</strong> sentence (one with its own tense). <em>That</em> is optional “only in the sense that we do not articulate it. It stays there” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> S + V + O, where O = <em>(that) I had a lot of money</em>. The same holds for <em>John knows (that) Peter likes Mary</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"finite-and-non-finite-clauses\">Finite and non-finite clauses</h3>\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a> (the embedded clause in brackets):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Non-finite clauses</th>\n<th>Finite clauses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I want [to go].</td>\n<td>I believe that [he is innocent].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I want [him to go].</td>\n<td>She said that [she can speak three languages].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I would like [to know what he did best].</td>\n<td>I wonder [what her name is].</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Could you tell [me] [if the doctor is available]?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Finite and non-finite clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An embedded clause with its own tense is <strong>finite</strong>. In all four finite examples the embedded clause happens to be in the present tense (<em>is</em>, <em>can</em>, <em>is</em>, <em>is</em>). <em>He is innocent</em> is “a complete sentence by itself”: it has a subject, a tense and a predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n<li>An embedded clause without tense is <strong>non-finite</strong>: <em>to go</em>, <em>him to go</em>. Such a clause “will not stand on its own”; it can only be part of a bigger <strong>matrix</strong> sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Who goes, in <em>I want to go</em>? The lecturer writes it as <em>I want [∅ to go]</em>, where <strong>∅</strong> marks a subject that is there but not said (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>I want to go</em> is really “I want I to go”.</li>\n<li>The subject of the matrix clause (<em>I</em>) and the subject of the embedded clause (<em>I</em>) refer to the same person, so the embedded subject is dropped: it becomes zero, ∅.</li>\n<li>In <em>I want him to go</em>, the subjects differ (<em>I</em> and <em>him</em>), so <em>him</em> must stay. “Only then the meaning becomes clearer.” The same holds for <em>I want her to go</em>, <em>I want them to go</em>, <em>I want them to come</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is the rule of <a href=\"#/english2/w03/sentence-and-clause-finite-and-non-finite\">Week 3</a>: <em>Raju wants to go home</em>, but <em>Raju wants Sarita to go home</em>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Embedding twice: <em>I would like to know what he did best</em> (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Matrix verb: <em>like</em>. It is transitive: you would like <strong>what</strong>?</li>\n<li>Its object is the non-finite clause <em>to know what he did best</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Know</em> is also transitive: to know <strong>what</strong>?</li>\n<li>Its object is another embedded clause, <em>what he did best</em>. (Being embedded, it has no fronting: not <em>what did he do best</em>.)</li>\n<li>So: <em>I would like [to know [what he did best]]</em>, a clause inside a clause inside the sentence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> embedding within an embedded clause. This is called <strong>recursion</strong>, “a very unique and specific property of human language”, which reflects a specific capacity of the human mind (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"which-sentences-are-complex\">Which sentences are complex?</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s rule: “when any embedded clause is the object of the verb, only such cases are called complex sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Complex?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I want to go.</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>a non-finite clause is the object of <em>want</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I want him to go.</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><em>him to go</em> is the object of <em>want</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I would like to know what he did best.</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><em>to know …</em> is the object of <em>like</em> (and contains another embedded clause)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju likes pizza and Ramu likes sandwich.</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>two coordinated sentences; the slide “Not a complex sentence …” drops the second verb: <em>Raju likes pizza and Ramu xxx sandwich</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>That Raju delivered a good lecture pleased Ramu.</td>\n<td>no, by this lecture</td>\n<td>the <em>that</em>-clause is the subject, not an object</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two definitions of “complex”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 6.6 says a <em>that</em>-clause in the subject position “does not make a sentence a complex one”; only an embedded clause in the object position does (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>). <a href=\"#/english2/w03/complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Week 3</a> (Lecture 3.1) used a wider definition: an independent clause with one or more subordinate clauses. By that definition, <em>That the pandemic would disappear was believed last year by everyone</em> (a noun clause as subject) is complex too. Know both, and say which one you are using.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"that-clauses-as-subjects\">That-clauses as subjects</h3>\n<p>The slide “that-Clause as Subjects of a Sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Predicate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. That Mary would forget John so quickly was rather a shock.</td>\n<td>the whole <em>that</em>-clause</td>\n<td>was rather a shock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. That Raju delivered a good lecture pleased Ramu.</td>\n<td>the whole <em>that</em>-clause</td>\n<td>pleased Ramu (<em>pleased</em> is the verb, <em>Ramu</em> its object)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. The fact that she didn’t recognize him was rather a shock.</td>\n<td><em>the fact</em> (the <em>that</em>-clause describes it)</td>\n<td>was rather a shock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. The idea that the leader should know everything is unacceptable.</td>\n<td><em>the idea</em> (the <em>that</em>-clause describes it)</td>\n<td>is unacceptable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. It surprised me that he was still in bed.</td>\n<td><em>it</em>, which stands for the <em>that</em>-clause</td>\n<td>surprised me</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. She made it clear that she wouldn’t accept the proposal.</td>\n<td><em>she</em> (<em>it</em> stands for the <em>that</em>-clause)</td>\n<td>made it clear …</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“That-clause is a subject only in these two sentences, one and two” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Substitution test (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To check whether a <em>that</em>-clause is the subject, replace the whole clause with a simple subject such as <em>it</em>. If the sentence is still good, the clause was the subject:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>That Mary would forget John so quickly was rather a shock.</em> → <em>It was rather a shock.</em></li>\n<li><em>That Raju delivered a good lecture pleased Ramu.</em> → <em>It pleased Ramu.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>The fact that she didn’t recognize him was rather a shock</em> (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Find the predicate: <em>was rather a shock</em>.</li>\n<li>Find the subject: the noun phrase before it, <em>the fact</em>.</li>\n<li><em>That she didn’t recognize him</em> tells us <strong>which</strong> fact: it modifies (describes) the noun <em>the fact</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the subject is <em>the fact</em>, not the <em>that</em>-clause. In the same way, in sentence 4 the subject is <em>the idea</em>, and <em>that the leader should know everything</em> modifies it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Making a heavy subject lighter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A whole sentence as subject is heavy. English uses <em>the idea that</em>, <em>the belief that</em> and <em>the fact that</em> (the slide “Introducing that-clause …”) “to reduce the heavy impact of a subject”. Instead of <em>That she did not recognize him was rather a shock</em>, we say <em>The fact that she did not recognize him was rather a shock</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>In sentence 5, <em>It surprised me that he was still in bed</em>, <em>it</em> is <strong>co-indexed</strong> with the <em>that</em>-clause: the two refer to the same thing. The clause “is basically pushed towards the right”, to the end, and <em>it</em> holds the subject place (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>). In sentence 6, <em>She made it clear that she wouldn’t accept the proposal</em>, <em>it</em> “again replaces” the clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>). Later, in the lecture’s object labels, the slide marks <em>me</em> in sentence 5 as the indirect object and the <em>that</em>-clause as the direct object (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>). Grammar books would not label it that way: <em>surprise</em> takes one object, <em>me</em>, and the <em>that</em>-clause is still the real subject, moved to the end, as above.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Seen before</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 3 had the same pattern: in <em>It was believed last year by everyone that the pandemic would disappear</em>, <em>it</em> only holds the place of the clause (<a href=\"#/english2/w03/complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Week 3</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"verbs-with-two-objects\">Verbs with two objects</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ditransitive verb with a that-clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>ditransitive</strong> verb takes two objects: an <strong>indirect object</strong> (IO), the person told or informed (to whom?), and a <strong>direct object</strong> (DO), what is told (what?). The second object can be a <em>that</em>-clause. The slide “Ditransitive verbs and that-clause” lists a handful: <em>tell, inform, advise, assure, convince, remind, promise, teach</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Indirect object</th>\n<th>Direct object</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>He told us that it would take a long time.</td>\n<td>us</td>\n<td>that it would take a long time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The principal convinced everyone that the course would be good for the class.</td>\n<td>everyone</td>\n<td>that the course would be good for the class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She convinced me that I was wrong.</td>\n<td>me</td>\n<td>that I was wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The pilot informed the passengers that the weather was not good.</td>\n<td>the passengers</td>\n<td>that the weather was not good</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“So, that-clause can also be used as an object of ditransitive verb” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-the-two-objects-go\">Where the two objects go</h3>\n<p>The slide “Direct Objects and Indirect Objects” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>). Here the direct object is short (light): <em>his response</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>OK?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. He emailed his response quickly.</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>the adverb at the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. He emailed quickly his response.</td>\n<td>acceptable (on the slide)</td>\n<td>“not a very good sentence”, because <em>quickly</em> should be at the end, “but still okay”. Most grammar books mark it wrong: an adverb does not go between a verb and a short object.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. He emailed his response to us.</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>an indirect object with <em>to</em> follows the direct object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. He emailed us his response.</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>an indirect object without a preposition can come first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. He emailed to us his response.</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>an indirect object with <em>to</em> cannot come before the direct object</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The next slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2400s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:00</a>). Now the direct object is a whole <em>that</em>-clause, which is <strong>heavy</strong>: a full sentence.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>OK?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. He informed that he had an urgent meeting to attend quickly.</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>the indirect object is missing (“IO missing” in ink)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. He informed quickly that he had an urgent meeting to attend.</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>the indirect object is missing; the place of <em>quickly</em> is not the problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. He informed that he had an urgent meeting to attend to us.</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>the heavy direct object must come last</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. He informed us that he had an urgent meeting to attend.</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>indirect object, then direct object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. He informed to us that he had an urgent meeting to attend.</td>\n<td>✓ (on the slide)</td>\n<td>the heavy direct object goes last, so even <em>to us</em> may come before it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Heavy elements go to the end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Heavy elements are only stacked towards the end in English sentences.” A <em>to</em>-phrase cannot come before a light direct object (✗ <em>He emailed to us his response</em>). But when the direct object is a full sentence, a heavy direct object, the indirect object with a preposition can come before it. Lecture 6.5 said the same of <em>whether</em> and <em>if</em> clauses: sentential complements come at the end.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The safest form with <em>inform</em></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Sentence 5 is accepted on the lecture’s slide, but standard English never puts <em>to</em> after <em>inform</em> (✗ <em>informed to us</em>): sentence 4, <em>He informed us that he had an urgent meeting to attend</em>, is the correct form. The heavy-object rule does work with verbs that take <em>to</em>, such as <em>explain</em>: <em>He explained to us that he had an urgent meeting to attend.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Things to notice …” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2550s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A complex sentence has an embedded sentence as an object of the verb in the matrix predicate.</li>\n<li>A coordinated sentence is not a complex sentence.</li>\n<li>In both subject and object positions we can have a sentence: still [S V O].</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So “a complex sentence is also an example of, essentially, a simple sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=81N4bFtxc7U&amp;t=2640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 44:00</a>).</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is the subject of <em>The fact that she didn’t recognize him was rather a shock</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>The fact.</strong> The <em>that</em>-clause, <em>that she didn’t recognize him</em>, modifies <em>the fact</em>, and the predicate is <em>was rather a shock</em>. Compare <em>That Mary would forget John so quickly was rather a shock</em>, where the whole <em>that</em>-clause is the subject.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Finite or non-finite? (a) <em>I want him to go.</em> (b) <em>I believe that he is innocent.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) <strong>Non-finite</strong>: <em>him to go</em> has no tense. (b) <strong>Finite</strong>: <em>he is innocent</em> has its own tense (present, <em>is</em>) and could stand alone as a sentence.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why is <em>He informed that he had an urgent meeting to attend</em> wrong, and how do you fix it?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Inform</em> takes two objects, and the indirect object, the person informed, is missing (“IO missing” on the slide). <strong>Fix:</strong> <em>He informed us that he had an urgent meeting to attend.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which is right: <em>He emailed to us his response</em> or <em>He emailed us his response</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>He emailed us his response.</em> An indirect object without a preposition can come before the direct object. With <em>to</em>, the indirect object must follow a light direct object: <em>He emailed his response to us</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Softening</strong> (6.1): accuracy (grammar) + appropriateness (fit to the context) = impactful speech; grammar comes first. Contexts: requests, suggestions and opinions, issues, refusal. Tools from the lecture: <em>please</em>; <em>Could we …?</em>; <em>I was wondering if …</em>; <em>I think …</em>; <em>If I were you, I would …</em>; <em>You seem to …</em>; <em>It appears to me that …</em>; <em>May I suggest …?</em>; <em>Hope you won’t mind</em>; saying no without <em>no</em>. <em>Please</em> alone does not make a request: context and hierarchy decide.</li>\n<li><strong>Negatives</strong> (6.2): the tense leaves the verb: tense + <em>not</em> + verb (<em>did not confirm</em>); <em>not</em> follows a tense word that is already outside (<em>was not</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Question tags</strong> (6.2): a comma, then a short question at the end. Same subject (as a pronoun), same tense (with agreement); affirmative → negative tag, negative (including <em>never</em>) → positive tag; contraction is compulsory (<em>don’t, isn’t, wasn’t, didn’t, doesn’t</em>). Functions: engage, verify, confirm. Avoid first-language tags such as <em>right?</em> and <em>isn’t it?</em></li>\n<li><strong>Agreement</strong> (6.3): person (I, II, III) and number (singular, plural) of the subject, matched on the verb; gender plays little part in English. <strong>Tense markers</strong>: <em>is/are/am</em>, <em>was/were</em>, <em>shall/will</em>; otherwise the tense is on the verb (<em>loves, loved</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Questions</strong> (6.3): tense + agreement are fronted; <em>do</em> carries them when no other tense word is present (<em>does</em> = do + present, <em>did</em> = do + past); the wh-word comes first. Content (wh-) and yes-or-no questions. Do not let a question sound like an interrogation.</li>\n<li><strong>Complex sentence</strong> (6.4, 6.6): an embedded sentence is the object of the verb in the matrix predicate. A coordinated sentence is not complex, and a complex sentence is still S + V + O.</li>\n<li><strong>Embedded questions</strong> (6.4): no extraction of tense: <em>I asked what you ate</em>, <em>I know what you wanted</em>, <em>I wonder what her name is</em>, <em>Could you tell me if the doctor is available?</em> They are less direct, so more polite.</li>\n<li><strong>Complementizers</strong> <em>that, whether, if</em> introduce complement clauses. <em>That</em> can be dropped before a finite object clause (not a subject clause). Only <em>whether</em>: before a <em>to</em>-infinitive, after a preposition, right before <em>or not</em>. Both, with <em>or</em> later in the sentence: <em>whether</em> formal, <em>if</em> informal. Only some predicates (<em>ask, tell, unsure</em>) take these clauses. Heavy (sentence) complements go to the end.</li>\n<li><strong>Finite</strong> clause: has its own tense. <strong>Non-finite</strong>: no tense (<em>to go</em>), cannot stand alone. An embedded subject that matches the matrix subject is dropped (<em>I want ∅ to go</em>, but <em>I want him to go</em>). A clause inside a clause is <strong>recursion</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Subjects</strong> (6.6): hidden <em>you</em> (imperatives); expletive <em>it, there</em> (subject must be overt); a noun phrase; a whole <em>that</em>-clause (test: <em>It pleased Ramu</em>). <em>The fact / idea / belief that …</em> lightens a heavy subject; in <em>It surprised me that …</em>, <em>it</em> stands for the clause, which moves to the end.</li>\n<li><strong>Two objects</strong> (<em>tell, inform, advise, assure, convince, remind, promise, teach</em>): indirect object (<em>us</em>) + direct object (a <em>that</em>-clause). A <em>to</em>-phrase cannot come before a light direct object (✗ <em>emailed to us his response</em>); a heavy direct object goes last.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Turn <em>Meet with me at 9 in the morning</em> into the lecture’s three softer versions, and say what each change does.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning?</em> The order becomes a question with <em>could</em>, and <em>me</em> becomes <em>we</em>. This is polite.</li>\n<li><em>Could we meet at 9 in the morning, please?</em> Now <em>please</em> is added. This is more polite: use it when you are not in a position to tell people to meet you.</li>\n<li><em>I was wondering if we could meet at 9 in the morning.</em> The question is wrapped inside a statement with <em>if</em> (an indirect question). This is “excessively polite”: it gives the hearer an option.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Add the tags: <em>You liked traveling, ___?</em> and <em>You did not like traveling, ___?</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>You liked traveling</em> is affirmative and past. Subject <em>you</em>; the past comes out as <em>did</em>; the tag is negative and contracted: <strong>didn’t you?</strong></li>\n<li><em>You did not like traveling</em> is negative; the past is already outside the verb (<em>did</em>). The tag is positive: <strong>did you?</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make <em>What is her name?</em> less direct, and explain why <em>is</em> does not move.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>I wonder what her name is.</em> The question becomes the object of <em>wonder</em>, so it is embedded. An embedded question is not a question any more, so tense and agreement are not extracted: <em>her name is</em>, not <em>is her name</em>. The sentence still shows your curiosity, but it does not ask directly.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find every clause in <em>I would like to know what he did best</em>, and name the pattern.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>Matrix clause: <em>I would like …</em>, with <em>like</em> transitive.</li>\n<li>Object of <em>like</em>: <em>to know what he did best</em>, a non-finite clause (no tense).</li>\n<li>Object of <em>know</em>: <em>what he did best</em>, a finite embedded clause (past, <em>did</em>), with no fronting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A clause inside a clause inside the sentence: <strong>recursion</strong>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is the subject of <em>That Raju delivered a good lecture pleased Ramu</em>, and is the sentence complex by Lecture 6.6’s rule?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The subject is the whole <em>that</em>-clause, <em>That Raju delivered a good lecture</em>. Test: replace it with <em>it</em>: <em>It pleased Ramu</em>. The predicate is <em>pleased Ramu</em>, where <em>Ramu</em> is the object. By Lecture 6.6’s rule the sentence is <strong>not complex</strong>, because the clause is the subject, not the object. (By Week 3’s wider definition it would count as complex.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which reply to <em>Sir, I have submitted my report on accounts. Hope you liked it.</em> is softer: <em>You have made a mistake in calculations</em> or <em>It appears to me that the calculations do not add up. Could you please take a look at it again?</em> Name the softening tools.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The second. It carries the same message without “devastating” the listener:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>It appears to me that …</em> presents the problem as the speaker’s view, not as a verdict (a <em>that</em>-clause after <em>appears to me</em>);</li>\n<li><em>Could you please …?</em> is a polite question in place of an order.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both replies are grammatical. Only the second is appropriate to the context.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
